  <!-- ========================================================================= -->
  <!-- PHASE 1: SIGNAL INGESTION & TRI-BRANCH FEATURE EXTRACTION -->
  <!-- ========================================================================= -->
  <div class="phase-section">
    <div class="phase-banner" style="border-left-color: var(--c-blue);">
      <div class="phase-eyebrow" style="color: var(--c-blue);">PHASE 1</div>
      <div class="phase-title">Signal Ingestion &amp; Tri-Branch Feature Extraction</div>
      <div class="phase-goal">GOAL: Transform continuous, unquantized audio into three complementary, time-synchronized 256-dimensional feature representations.</div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 1 -->
    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-num-badge">1</div>
        <div class="step-title-block">
          <div class="step-heading">Continuous Audio Ingestion via GPU Circular Ring Buffer</div>
          <div class="step-tensor-sig">Tensor Progression: Stream → Buffer Tensor [B=1, Channels=1, Samples=160,000]</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid-2col">
        <div class="content-box">
          <div class="block-label lbl-how">HOW THE SYSTEM EXECUTES THIS</div>
          <ul class="bullet-list">
            <li><strong>Memory Allocation:</strong> A contiguous array of size <code>(1, 160000)</code> is pre-allocated in GPU pinned memory, representing 10.0 seconds of 16.0 kHz mono PCM audio.</li>
            <li><strong>Rolling Shift (Stride: 2.0 s):</strong> Every 2.0 seconds, the ingestion daemon reads the newest 32,000 samples from the audio driver, discards the oldest 32,000 samples in the buffer via an in-place shift, and appends the new audio.</li>
            <li><strong>Overlap Guarantee:</strong> An 80% temporal overlap is maintained across consecutive inference windows, ensuring that speech events are evaluated multiple times across different temporal positions.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="content-box">
          <div class="block-label lbl-why">FORENSIC &amp; ENGINEERING RATIONALE</div>
          <ul class="bullet-list">
            <li><strong>Zero-Copy PCIe Transfer:</strong> Pinned memory DMA transfers avoid CPU↔GPU memory copying bottlenecks, executing the complete buffer update in <strong>&lt; 3.8 ms</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>Eliminating Splicing Blind Spots:</strong> Threat actors often deploy "partial deepfakes" (inserting a 1.5-second cloned phrase into an authentic 2-minute call). An 80% overlap guarantees a short synthetic phrase cannot slip into an unobserved gap between frames.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="analogy-card">
        <strong>Beginner Analogy: The Dashcam Principle</strong>
        Imagine a car dashcam that continuously keeps the last 10 minutes of driving. It doesn't record forever until its SD card runs out of space; it constantly erases the oldest minute as a new minute is recorded. This ensures the AI always has 10 seconds of full conversational context without its memory ever crashing.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 2 -->
    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-num-badge">2</div>
        <div class="step-title-block">
          <div class="step-heading">Tri-Branch Acoustic Extraction (Parallel Front-End Bifurcation)</div>
          <div class="step-tensor-sig">Input: [B, 1, 160000] → Bifurcates into 3 Heterogeneous Representations</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid-3col">
        <!-- Branch A -->
        <div class="content-box" style="border-top: 3px solid var(--c-blue);">
          <span class="branch-tag tag-blue">BRANCH A · Physical Linear STFT</span>
          <div class="block-label lbl-how">INTERNAL MECHANICS</div>
          <ul class="bullet-list">
            <li>Executes a GPU Short-Time Fourier Transform: <code>N_fft = 2048</code>, hop size <code>H = 160</code> (10 ms step), window length <code>W = 2048</code> (Hann window).</li>
            <li>Extracts the absolute magnitude spectrum:
              <div class="math-block">|X(m, k)| = |∑ x(n + mH) w(n) e^{-j 2π k n / N}|</div>
            </li>
            <li>Yields a linear frequency matrix of shape: <code>[B, 1025, 1000]</code>.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="block-label lbl-why" style="margin-top:14px;">WHY WE NEED IT</div>
          <p style="font-size:12.5px; color:var(--text-body);">
            Neural vocoders (e.g., HiFi-GAN, WaveNet) synthesize speech in rigid mathematical frames, leaving microscopic high-frequency "checkerboard artifacts" and spectral grid lines above 4000 Hz. The linear STFT treats all frequency bins with equal physical resolution, exposing these uncompressed digital seams.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Branch B -->
        <div class="content-box" style="border-top: 3px solid var(--c-amber);">
          <span class="branch-tag tag-amber">BRANCH B · Perceptual Log-Mel</span>
          <div class="block-label lbl-how">INTERNAL MECHANICS</div>
          <ul class="bullet-list">
            <li>Multiplies the STFT magnitude matrix by a static GPU sparse Mel filterbank matrix: <code>M_filterbank ∈ ℝ^(1025 × 128)</code> covering 0 to 8000 Hz.</li>
            <li>Applies logarithmic dynamic range scaling:
              <div class="math-block">S_mel = log(|X| · M_filterbank + 1e-6)</div>
            </li>
            <li>Yields a perceptual Mel matrix of shape: <code>[B, 128, 1000]</code>.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="block-label lbl-why" style="margin-top:14px;">WHY WE NEED IT</div>
          <p style="font-size:12.5px; color:var(--text-body);">
            The human ear resolves low pitches with high precision but compresses high pitches. By warping frequencies logarithmically via <span class="inline-code">m = 2595 log10(1 + f/700)</span>, this branch zooms in on the 0–4000 Hz range, providing dense representations of vocal tract formants, vowel resonance, and speech prosody.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Branch C -->
        <div class="content-box" style="border-top: 3px solid var(--c-purple);">
          <span class="branch-tag tag-purple">BRANCH C · Parametric SincNet</span>
          <div class="block-label lbl-how">INTERNAL MECHANICS</div>
          <ul class="bullet-list">
            <li>Processes the raw waveform directly through 70 learnable 1D sinc bandpass filters:
              <div class="math-block">g(n, f1, f2) = 2 f2 sinc(2π f2 n) - 2 f1 sinc(2π f1 n)</div>
            </li>
            <li>Cutoff frequencies <span class="inline-code">[f_low, f_high]</span> are updated via backpropagation during training.</li>
            <li>Yields a raw sub-band feature map of shape: <code>[B, 70, 1000]</code>.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="block-label lbl-why" style="margin-top:14px;">WHY WE NEED IT</div>
          <p style="font-size:12.5px; color:var(--text-body);">
            Spectrograms only capture magnitude, completely discarding wave phase. SincNet operates directly on raw oscillating pressure waves, catching micro-timing phase jumps, frame-boundary clicking, and vocoder phase mismatch that are completely invisible on spectrogram images.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 3 -->
    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-num-badge">3</div>
        <div class="step-title-block">
          <div class="step-heading">Latent Space Projection &amp; 2D Spatial Attention Gating</div>
          <div class="step-tensor-sig">Shape Harmonization: Heterogeneous Inputs → 3 × [B, 1000, 256]</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid-2col">
        <div class="content-box">
          <div class="block-label lbl-how">PROJECTION OPERATIONS</div>
          <ul class="bullet-list">
            <li><strong>Linear Stream Projection:</strong> Convolved via <code>nn.Conv2d(1, 256, kernel_size=(1025, 1))</code> followed by <code>BatchNorm2d</code> and <code>GELU</code> activation → <code>X_lin ∈ ℝ^(B × 1000 × 256)</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Mel Stream Projection:</strong> Convolved via <code>nn.Conv2d(1, 256, kernel_size=(128, 1))</code> followed by <code>BatchNorm2d</code> and <code>GELU</code> activation → <code>X_mel ∈ ℝ^(B × 1000 × 256)</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>SincNet 2D Attention Mask:</strong> Passes through a spatial convolutional gate to compute mask <span class="inline-code">M ∈ [0, 1]</span>:
              <div class="math-block">X_sinc = (M ⊗ Sinc_features) · W_proj ∈ ℝ^(B × 1000 × 256)</div>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="content-box">
          <div class="block-label lbl-why">WHY PROJECTION IS ESSENTIAL</div>
          <ul class="bullet-list">
            <li><strong>Dimensional Alignment:</strong> The three branches produce wildly different channel counts (1025 linear bins vs. 128 Mel bands vs. 70 Sinc bands). Projecting all streams into a unified latent space (<span class="inline-code">d_model = 256</span>) allows downstream cross-attention layers to perform direct dot-product alignment.</li>
            <li><strong>Silence &amp; Noise Suppression:</strong> The 2D attention mask acts like an automatic noise gate, dampening non-vocal background hiss and pauses so silence does not contaminate the embedding.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========================================================================= -->
  <!-- PHASE 2: TRI-STREAM BIDIRECTIONAL MAMBA (BIMAMBA) SEQUENCE MODELING -->
  <!-- ========================================================================= -->
  <div class="phase-section">
    <div class="phase-banner" style="border-left-color: var(--c-amber);">
      <div class="phase-eyebrow" style="color: var(--c-amber);">PHASE 2</div>
      <div class="phase-title">Tri-Stream Bidirectional Mamba Sequence Modeling</div>
      <div class="phase-goal">GOAL: Model long-range non-causal temporal context and speech rhythm across time in linear complexity O(N), replacing quadratic Transformer self-attention.</div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 4 -->
    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-num-badge">4</div>
        <div class="step-title-block">
          <div class="step-heading">Dynamic Parameter Discretization &amp; Input-Dependent Noise Gating</div>
          <div class="step-tensor-sig">Mathematical Selective State-Space Mechanics inside each Mamba Block</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid-2col">
        <div class="content-box">
          <div class="block-label lbl-math">CONTINUOUS-TO-DISCRETE STATE EQUATIONS</div>
          <p style="font-size:13px; margin-bottom:8px;">A standard continuous state space model maps an input sequence $x(t)$ to latent state $h(t)$:</p>
          <div class="math-block">
            h'(t) = A h(t) + B x(t)<br>
            y(t) = C h(t)
          </div>
          <p style="font-size:13px; margin-bottom:8px;">Mamba discretizes these equations using an input-dependent step size $\Delta_t$:</p>
          <div class="math-block">
            A_bar = exp(Δ_t · A)<br>
            B_bar = (Δ_t · B_t)<br>
            h_t = A_bar · h_{t-1} + B_bar · x_t<br>
            y_t = C_t · h_t
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="content-box">
          <div class="block-label lbl-failsafe">HOW MAMBA MATHEMATICALLY GATES OUT NOISE</div>
          <ul class="bullet-list">
            <li><strong>Dynamic Parameter Projections:</strong> Unlike traditional RNNs or CNNs with fixed weights, Mamba computes parameters dynamically from the input frame:
              <div class="math-block">B_t = Linear(x_t), \quad C_t = Linear(x_t), \quad Δ_t = Softplus(Linear(x_t))</div>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Zeroing the Input Gain on Noise:</strong> When an incoming frame $x_t$ contains stationary background noise, room static, or silence, the projection network sets $B_t \to 0$ and $\Delta_t \to 0$.</li>
            <li><strong>Memory Isolation:</strong> Because $B_{\text{bar}} \to 0$, the state update becomes $h_t \approx h_{t-1} + 0$. <strong>Background noise is multiplied by zero and blocked from entering the memory state $h_t$.</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="analogy-card">
        <strong>Beginner Analogy: The Intelligent Water Valve</strong>
        Think of traditional AI layers like an open pipe: whenever dirty water (noise) or clean water (speech) flows in, it all mixes together. Mamba acts like an ultra-fast automated valve: the instant it senses dirty water, it closes the valve completely ($B_t \to 0$), holding only clean water in its reservoir until speech returns.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 5, 6, 7 -->
    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-num-badge">5-7</div>
        <div class="step-title-block">
          <div class="step-heading">Dual-Direction Sweeps: Forward Carryover + Backward Zero-Init</div>
          <div class="step-tensor-sig">Execution: Parallel forward/backward sweeps across all 3 branches → H_branch ∈ [B, 1000, 256]</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid-2col">
        <div class="content-box">
          <div class="block-label lbl-how">SWEEP 1: FORWARD MAMBA (STREAMING CONTINUITY)</div>
          <ul class="bullet-list">
            <li><strong>Scan Direction:</strong> Scans chronological frames from $t = 1 \to 1000$.</li>
            <li><strong>Trans-Chunk State Carryover:</strong> To maintain seamless conversational memory across hours of live calls, the initial state $h_0$ inherits the final state $h_{1000}^{\text{prev}}$ from the preceding 10-second window.</li>
            <li><strong>Forensic Purpose:</strong> Tracks long-range prosodic drift, speaking tempo changes, and unnatural cadence breaks across the complete phone call.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="content-box">
          <div class="block-label lbl-how">SWEEP 2: BACKWARD MAMBA (LOCAL NON-CAUSAL CONTEXT)</div>
          <ul class="bullet-list">
            <li><strong>Scan Direction:</strong> Flips the 1000-frame sequence and scans in reverse from $t = 1000 \to 1$.</li>
            <li><strong>Zero-Initialization:</strong> The backward initial state is strictly reset to zero ($h'_{\text{init}} = 0$) for every 10-second window.</li>
            <li><strong>Forensic Purpose:</strong> Speech is context-dependent. Knowing what sound follows a phoneme reveals whether a 40ms pause was a natural breath or an artificial vocoder frame splice. Zero-init prevents illegal future data leakage from unrecorded audio.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="deep-dive-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-title">Residual Sweep Concatenation (Step 7)</div>
        <p style="font-size:13px; color:var(--text-body); margin-bottom:8px;">
          The outputs of the forward and reversed state-space sweeps are concatenated along the channel dimension ($512$ channels), projected back to $256$ dimensions, and stabilized with a residual skip connection:
        </p>
        <div class="math-block">
          H_{\text{branch}} = \text{Linear}\Big(\big[Y_{\text{forward}} \parallel \text{Flip}(Y_{\text{backward}})\big]\Big) + X_{\text{branch}} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times 1000 \times 256}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========================================================================= -->
  <!-- PHASE 3: CROSS-STREAM FUSION & DYNAMIC GATING -->
  <!-- ========================================================================= -->
  <div class="phase-section">
    <div class="phase-banner" style="border-left-color: var(--c-purple);">
      <div class="phase-eyebrow" style="color: var(--c-purple);">PHASE 3</div>
      <div class="phase-title">Cross-Stream Multi-Modal Fusion &amp; Dynamic Gating</div>
      <div class="phase-goal">GOAL: Cross-examine physical and perceptual features through Mutual Cross-Attention and dynamically recalibrate channels under real-world attack conditions.</div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 8 -->
    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-num-badge">8</div>
        <div class="step-title-block">
          <div class="step-heading">Multi-Stream Mutual Cross-Attention (MCA) Alignment</div>
          <div class="step-tensor-sig">Symmetric Cross-Query Verification across Physical, Perceptual &amp; Sub-Band Streams</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid-2col">
        <div class="content-box">
          <div class="block-label lbl-math">MCA MATHEMATICAL FORMULATION</div>
          <p style="font-size:13px; margin-bottom:8px;">Unlike unidirectional attention, MCA establishes a symmetric information exchange:</p>
          <div class="math-block">
            Q_{\text{lin}} = H_{\text{lin}} W_{Q1}, \quad K_{\text{mel}} = H_{\text{mel}} W_{K1}, \quad V_{\text{mel}} = H_{\text{mel}} W_{V1}<br>
            \text{Attn}_{\text{Lin}\to\text{Mel}} = \text{Softmax}\left(\frac{Q_{\text{lin}} K_{\text{mel}}^T}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right) V_{\text{mel}}
          </div>
          <div class="math-block">
            Q_{\text{mel}} = H_{\text{mel}} W_{Q2}, \quad K_{\text{lin}} = H_{\text{lin}} W_{K2}, \quad V_{\text{lin}} = H_{\text{lin}} W_{V2}<br>
            \text{Attn}_{\text{Mel}\to\text{Lin}} = \text{Softmax}\left(\frac{Q_{\text{mel}} K_{\text{lin}}^T}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right) V_{\text{lin}}
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="content-box">
          <div class="block-label lbl-why">WHY SYMMETRIC CROSS-ATTENTION WORKS</div>
          <ul class="bullet-list">
            <li><strong>The Detective Negotiation:</strong> Synthetic voice engines rarely fool physical and perceptual domains simultaneously.
              <br>• <em>Linear-to-Mel:</em> The Linear branch queries the Mel branch to verify if a suspicious high-frequency vocoder seam aligns with an unnatural vocal tract formant shift.
              <br>• <em>Mel-to-Linear:</em> The Mel branch queries the Linear branch to verify if an abnormal prosodic pause is backed by physical vocoder frame discontinuities.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Sinc Sub-Band Integration:</strong> The SincNet sequence cross-attends against the concatenated spectral features, anchoring time-domain phase data against frequency formants.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 9 -->
    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-num-badge">9</div>
        <div class="step-title-block">
          <div class="step-heading">Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) Adaptive Channel Recalibration</div>
          <div class="step-tensor-sig">Dynamic Feature Gating: Generating Fused Latent Tensor Z ∈ [B, 1000, 256]</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid-2col">
        <div class="content-box">
          <div class="block-label lbl-how">SE GATING COMPUTATION</div>
          <ul class="bullet-list">
            <li>The cross-attended representations are concatenated and passed into an SE recalibration block.</li>
            <li>Global average pooling calculates a channel descriptor vector:
              <div class="math-block">s = \text{AdaptiveAvgPool}(Z_{\text{concat}}) \in \mathbb{R}^{256}</div>
            </li>
            <li>Two fully connected layers with Sigmoid activation compute adaptive channel weights:
              <div class="math-block">g = \sigma\Big(W_2 \cdot \text{GELU}(W_1 s)\Big) \in \mathbb{R}^{256}</div>
            </li>
            <li>Output: <span class="inline-code">Z = g ⊗ Z_concat ∈ ℝ^(B × 1000 × 256)</span>.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="content-box">
          <div class="block-label lbl-failsafe">THE REAL-WORLD FAIL-SAFE MECHANISM</div>
          <ul class="bullet-list">
            <li><strong>Replay &amp; Codec Protection:</strong> If an attacker plays deepfake audio through a cheap phone speaker (replay attack) or sends it over WhatsApp/Opus codecs, high-frequency linear details are destroyed.</li>
            <li><strong>Automatic Branch Switching:</strong> Under clean conditions, the SE gate weights Linear and Mel channels equally. Under heavy compression or room reverberation, the SE gate automatically drives linear channel weights to zero ($g_{\text{lin}} \to 0$) and increases the weights of Mel and prosodic channels ($g_{\text{mel}} \to 1$).</li>
            <li>The system gracefully falls back to speech rhythm and formant dynamics without manual reconfiguration.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========================================================================= -->
  <!-- PHASE 4: CLASSIFICATION & REAL-TIME TEMPORAL SMOOTHING -->
  <!-- ========================================================================= -->
  <div class="phase-section">
    <div class="phase-banner" style="border-left-color: var(--c-teal);">
      <div class="phase-eyebrow" style="color: var(--c-teal);">PHASE 4</div>
      <div class="phase-title">Classification &amp; Real-Time Temporal Smoothing</div>
      <div class="phase-goal">GOAL: Aggregate sequence frames into a global representation, classify window spoof probability, and eliminate edge jitter via asymmetric filtering.</div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 10 & 11 -->
    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-num-badge">10-11</div>
        <div class="step-title-block">
          <div class="step-heading">Attentive Statistics Pooling &amp; Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) Classifier</div>
          <div class="step-tensor-sig">Tensor Progression: Z [B, 1000, 256] → Vector z [B, 256] → Raw Window Probability P_k ∈ [0, 1]</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid-2col">
        <div class="content-box">
          <div class="block-label lbl-how">ATTENTIVE STATISTICS POOLING</div>
          <p style="font-size:13px; margin-bottom:8px;">Deepfake glitches often occur in 200ms bursts rather than spreading across the entire 10 seconds. Standard average pooling washes out these brief clues. Attentive pooling calculates dynamic frame importance weights $e_t$:</p>
          <div class="math-block">
            e_t = \text{Softmax}\big(v^T \tanh(W Z_t + b)\big)<br>
            \mu = \sum_{t=1}^{1000} e_t Z_t, \quad \sigma = \sqrt{\sum_{t=1}^{1000} e_t (Z_t - \mu)^2}<br>
            z = \text{Linear}\big([\mu \parallel \sigma]\big) \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times 256}
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="content-box">
          <div class="block-label lbl-how">MLP CLASSIFICATION HEAD</div>
          <ul class="bullet-list">
            <li>The global embedding $z$ passes through a regularized classification head:
              <div class="math-block">
                z_1 = \text{GELU}(\text{Linear}(256, 64) \cdot z)<br>
                z_2 = \text{Dropout}(p = 0.30)(z_1)<br>
                [P_{\text{real}}, P_{\text{fake}}] = \text{Softmax}(\text{Linear}(64, 2) \cdot z_2)
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>Dropout ($p=0.30$) on the lightweight head prevents memorizing specific vocoder profiles, maximizing generalization to unseen voice generators.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 12 & 13 -->
    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-header">
        <div class="step-num-badge">12-13</div>
        <div class="step-title-block">
          <div class="step-heading">Gaussian Center-Weighting &amp; Asymmetric Exponential Moving Average (EMA)</div>
          <div class="step-tensor-sig">Output: Stabilized, Low-Latency Real-Time Risk Score S_t ∈ [0, 1]</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid-2col">
        <div class="content-box">
          <div class="block-label lbl-how">GAUSSIAN WINDOW WEIGHTING (STEP 12)</div>
          <p style="font-size:13px; margin-bottom:8px;">STFT and sequence edges ($t = 0\text{--}2\text{ s}$ and $t = 8\text{--}10\text{ s}$) are prone to window truncation artifacts. We apply a Gaussian bell curve centered at $t = 5.0\text{ s}$ ($\sigma = 2.0\text{ s}$):</p>
          <div class="math-block">w(t) = \exp\left(-\frac{(t - 5.0)^2}{2 \sigma^2}\right)</div>
          <p style="font-size:12.5px; color:var(--text-body);">Down-weights edge frames and forces the model to rely on center frames ($t = 3\text{--}7\text{ s}$) where bidirectional context is maximal.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-box">
          <div class="block-label lbl-failsafe">ASYMMETRIC EMA FILTER (STEP 13)</div>
          <p style="font-size:13px; margin-bottom:8px;">Updates the running real-time risk score $S_t$ across consecutive 2.0-second inference strides:</p>
          <div class="math-block">S_t = \alpha \cdot P_k + (1 - \alpha) \cdot S_{t-1}</div>
          <ul class="bullet-list">
            <li><strong>Fast Attack ($\alpha = 0.75$):</strong> When $P_k > S_{t-1}$ (spoof detected), the filter reacts within $180\text{ ms}$, immediately spiking the UI gauge.</li>
            <li><strong>Slow Decay ($\alpha = 0.15$):</strong> When $P_k \le S_{t-1}$ (benign voice), the score decays gradually, preventing attackers from clearing an active alarm by pausing speech.</li>
            <li><strong>Instant Trigger Override:</strong> If $P_k > 0.88$, the EMA is bypassed completely for instant threat lockdown.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

